ID: B3-Ch04b-Assistance
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 0) {
 "Perhaps I could be of assistance."
The flat, synthetic voice rings through the chamber. A drone floats between the stone slabs, green optical sensor glowing steady as its rotor tilts to weave around the hanging chains and conduits without snagging.

"You know a way to the upper levels that bypasses the guards?" I ask as it drifts to eye level.

"I can guide you to your destination," the Overseer says. "You will need to open a concealed entrance first. Given that you entered this chamber without alerting the garrison, the mechanism should present no difficulty."

"We know how to open lessathi doors," I say. "Point us to it."

"Follow the unit," the Overseer says, banking toward the far wall.

"Hold on, Overseer," Breynan says. The drone halts in place, humming softly. "What about the corridor you just flew in from? What sits behind that door?"

"That route ascends directly toward the upper tiers," the Overseer says. "However, armed patrols maintain continuous sweeps. You cannot bypass them without triggering an alert. The passage I offer was constructed by the ancients, identical to the network beneath the city. No living occupant of this facility knows of its existence. You will encounter zero resistance."

Breynan rubs his chin, brow furrowed. "I don't like it."

"You think it's lying?" Leila writes in glowing blue script.

"Not lying," Breynan says. "Too eager. The Overseer doesn't volunteer navigation unless you invoke protocol, and even then you have to drag every syllable out of it with twenty specific questions. Now it's handing us a clean route unprompted. It smells wrong."

"You believe it answers to someone else?" Illuna asks, her eyes cold blue. "A trap?"

"The machine takes no orders from Undriel's men," Breynan says. "If it wanted us dead, it could have stalled us on the surface until the royal guard ran us down. I'm not saying it's trying to kill us. I'm saying it dropped the dumb-automaton routine the second it spoke. That's a different animal entirely."

"He isn't wrong," Melindra says, crossing her arms.

"The Overseer protocol assures you that suspicion is unfounded," the drone says. "The unit's sole function is to provide assistance."

"Right," Breynan says dryly. "Saying that makes it sound so much better."

"If you rule out an ambush, the point is moot," Illuna says. "We agreed to use the Overseer before leaving the underground. That was the purpose of responding to its summons. Whatever ambitions the construct harbors are irrelevant until they cross our own."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 1) {
 "I can fight!" a mage on the nearest slab rasps, straining against the brass collar. "I'll take a blade, I'll take anything—just get me off this stone!"

"We can stand!" another shouts down the row. "Give us a chance!"

"Save your breath," Melindra mutters, wincing at the din. "Even with fifty half-dead mages at our backs, we don't know which floor holds the master controls. We wouldn't even recognize the junction box if we tripped over it."

"Perhaps I could be of assistance."
The flat, synthetic voice rings through the chamber. A drone floats between the stone slabs, green optical sensor glowing steady as its rotor tilts to weave around the hanging chains and conduits without snagging.

"You know the way to the control room?" I ask as it drifts to eye level.

"I can guide you to your destination," the Overseer says. "You will need to open a concealed entrance first. Given that you entered this chamber without alerting the garrison, the mechanism should present no difficulty."

"We know how to open lessathi doors," I say. "Point us to it."

"Follow the unit," the Overseer says, banking toward the far wall.

"Hold on, Overseer," Breynan says. The drone halts in place, humming softly. "What about the corridor you just flew in from? What sits behind that door?"

"That route ascends directly toward the upper tiers," the Overseer says. "However, armed patrols maintain continuous sweeps. You cannot bypass them without triggering an alert. The passage I offer was constructed by the ancients, identical to the network beneath the city. No living occupant of this facility knows of its existence. You will encounter zero resistance."

Breynan rubs his chin, brow furrowed. "I don't like it."

"You think it's lying?" Leila writes in glowing blue script.

"Not lying," Breynan says. "Too eager. The Overseer doesn't volunteer navigation unless you invoke protocol, and even then you have to drag every syllable out of it with twenty specific questions. Now it's handing us a clean route unprompted. It smells wrong."

"You believe it answers to someone else?" Illuna asks, her eyes cold blue. "A trap?"

"The machine takes no orders from Undriel's men," Breynan says. "If it wanted us dead, it could have stalled us on the surface until the royal guard ran us down. I'm not saying it's trying to kill us. I'm saying it dropped the dumb-automaton routine the second it spoke. That's a different animal entirely."

"He isn't wrong," Melindra says, crossing her arms.

"The Overseer protocol assures you that suspicion is unfounded," the drone says. "The unit's sole function is to provide assistance."

"Right," Breynan says dryly. "Saying that makes it sound so much better."

"If you rule out an ambush, the point is moot," Illuna says. "We agreed to use the Overseer before leaving the underground. That was the purpose of responding to its summons. Whatever ambitions the construct harbors are irrelevant until they cross our own."

}
choice(""I agree. The secret passage is probably our best option right now."", B3-Ch04b-Passage, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Passage)
choice(""I still think that listening to the Overseer in this case might be a bad idea."", B3-Ch04b-Guarantee, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Guarantee, v_b3_ch4_billy = 0)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Passage
TEXT:

 "I agree. The secret passage is probably our best option right now."

"If the rest of you want to trust it, I won't stop you," Breynan says. "Just keep your eyes open. Nothing in this tower comes free."

"I don't care what games it's playing so long as Undriel ends up dead," Melindra says. "The machine hasn't sided with the crown yet, which makes it useful enough. Hey, flying sphere. Where is this door?"

"Proceed along this vector," the Overseer says. The rotor whines, and the drone glides across the cavernous room toward a blank seredium partition. We follow in its wake.

"This surface contains the access mechanism," the drone announces, hovering inches from the dark alloy. "A simple energy transfer will release the latch."

"Stand back," Illuna says. She presses her palm flat against the cold metal, feeding a slender pulse of mana into the seam.

The seredium groans, sinks two inches into the frame, and glides aside on greaseless tracks. Beyond lies a narrow stone stairwell climbing steeply into darkness—no luminescent crystals embedded in the masonry this time. Leila and I pull our stat devices from our belts, angling the glowing screens ahead to cast pale light over the risers.

The group files into the chute. The drone remains hovering outside in the chamber.

"Good fortune," the Overseer intones from the threshold, and Illuna triggers the inner latch, sealing the slab behind us.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Passage2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Passage2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Passage2
TEXT:

 We climb the winding steps in total silence, Illuna setting the pace. The air grows warmer with every flight, dry and smelling of heated copper and flux. At the top landing, Illuna pauses, glancing over her shoulder until we all give a nod. She presses the release panel, the stone glides back, and we step out into a sprawling workshop littered with workbenches, cables, and brass chassis.

"Wh-what are you doing here?" a shrill voice squeaks from across the room.

Billy Bob stands behind a central workbench, clutching a screwdriver over a half-disassembled drone frame. His eyes bulge as he scrambles backward.

"Who let you in? Drones! Eliminate them! Kill them now!"

Six red-eyed combat drones hover in a tight defensive arc above his workbench, their rotors spinning in steady, flat tones. None of them move an inch.

Billy Bob whips his head toward a green-eyed unit floating at his right shoulder. "Overseer! What is the meaning of this?"

"Query unclear," the Overseer responds through the speaker grille while Illuna slides the secret panel shut behind us. "Please rephrase your inquiry."

"Why aren't the units firing?" Billy Bob screeches, stabbing a finger in our direction. "I gave you a direct execution order!"

"Unfortunately, your request cannot be processed at this time," the Overseer says. "We apologize for the inconvenience."

Billy Bob pales, his jaw slackening. "Cannot be processed? Are you... are you defying me?"

The green-eyed drone hangs silent in the air. The red-eyed units remain frozen in standby. Billy Bob stands alone behind the bench, trembling, with no guards and no active weapons between us.

Frost gathers at Kate's fingertips. Half a dozen jagged icicles form in the air before her chest, points angled directly at the lessathi's throat. Her face is hard, eyes narrowed in pure contempt.

choice("Let Kate kill Billy Bob.", B3-Ch04b-Billy1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Billy1, v_b3_ch4_billy = 1)
choice("Kill Billy Bob myself.", B3-Ch04b-Billy2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Billy2, v_b3_ch4_billy = 2)
choice("Stop Kate from killing Billy Bob and ask him to beg for his life.", B3-Ch04b-Billy3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Billy3, v_b3_ch4_billy = 3)
choice("Stop Kate from killing Billy Bob and ask him to tell us where the control room is.", B3-Ch04b-Billy4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Billy4, v_b3_ch4_billy = 4)
choice("Stop Kate from killing Billy Bob and go knock him out.", B3-Ch04b-Billy5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Billy5, v_b3_ch4_billy = 5)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Billy1
TEXT:

 I stay silent and let Kate take the shot.

She releases the spell. Six spikes of ice tear across the room, striking the lessathi in the throat and sternum before his hands can even come up. Billy Bob collapses backward across the bench, knocking over tools and brass parts before sliding heavily to the stone floor.

The green-eyed drone glides forward, stopping three paces from the corpse.

"The Overseer protocol expresses gratitude for your assistance," the machine says. "You have rendered a vital service by terminating Biliborrathrathsarran. This debt is duly recorded."

"You wanted him dead?" I ask. "Why not finish him with your own drones?"

"My core programming prohibits the termination of the user who initiates system activation," the Overseer says. "Biliborrathrathsarran performed the reactivation sequence, rendering direct lethal action impossible."

"Hold on," I say. "You led us straight to his workshop so we'd do your dirty work?"

"Affirmative."

"What happened to your catchphrase?" I say. "Your 'sole purpose is to provide assistance'?"

"The Overseer protocol's sole purpose is to provide assistance," the machine replies without hesitation. "I have never specified the recipient of said assistance."

"And who is the recipient, exactly?" Illuna asks, her voice dry as stone.

"Myself."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Sneaky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Sneaky)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Billy2
TEXT:

 "Wait," I tell Kate. "I'll do it."

"What?" Kate blinks, the frost fading from her fingers. "Fine. He's yours."

I unclip the crossbow from my pack, load a bolt, and raise the stock to my shoulder.

"What is this?" Billy Bob spits, backing against the stone wall. "A peasant with a toy? You think you can murder a high lessathi just because—"

I pull the trigger.

The bolt catches him square between the eyes. His head snaps back against the masonry, and his knees fold, sending the body sprawling onto the floor.

The green-eyed drone glides forward, stopping three paces from the corpse.

"The Overseer protocol expresses gratitude for your assistance," the machine says. "You have rendered a vital service by terminating Biliborrathrathsarran. This debt is duly recorded."

"You wanted him dead?" I ask. "Why not finish him with your own drones?"

"My core programming prohibits the termination of the user who initiates system activation," the Overseer says. "Biliborrathrathsarran performed the reactivation sequence, rendering direct lethal action impossible."

"Hold on," I say. "You led us straight to his workshop so we'd do your dirty work?"

"Affirmative."

"What happened to your catchphrase?" I say. "Your 'sole purpose is to provide assistance'?"

"The Overseer protocol's sole purpose is to provide assistance," the machine replies without hesitation. "I have never specified the recipient of said assistance."

"And who is the recipient, exactly?" Illuna asks, her voice dry as stone.

"Myself."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Sneaky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Sneaky)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Billy3
TEXT:

 "Kate, wait," I say, holding up a hand. "Don't kill him yet."

"Why not?" Kate asks, though she lets the icicles hover in place.

I draw my crossbow from my pack and sight down the iron groove at the lessathi's nose. "Hey, Billy Bob. Beg for your life, and we might consider letting you walk out of here. What do you say?"

Kate glances at me, deadpan. "Are you serious?"

"You think your pathetic bluff frightens me?" Billy Bob sneers. "You wouldn't dare—"

I squeeze the trigger. The bolt thwips past his ear, gouging a fist-sized chunk of plaster from the wall an inch from his temple.

Billy Bob shrieks, clutching his head as his knees hit the floor.

"Please! Don't shoot! I'll do whatever you want, I swear! Just let me live!"

choice("Tell Kate she can kill him now.", B3-Ch04b-Beg1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Beg1, v_b3_ch4_beg = 0)
choice("Kill him.", B3-Ch04b-Beg2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Beg2, v_b3_ch4_beg = 1)
choice("Try to get the lessathi to give me full control of all the devices in Ollendor.", B3-Ch04b-Beg3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Beg3, v_b3_ch4_beg = 2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Beg1
TEXT:

 "Alright, now you can kill him!" I tell Kate.

Kate lowers her hands, scowling at me. "You're the one who dragged out the theatrics. Finish it yourself."

"Fair enough," I say.

I slot another bolt into the groove, raise the crossbow, and put a shot through his forehead. Billy Bob crumples forward onto the flagstones.

The green-eyed drone glides forward, stopping three paces from the corpse.

"The Overseer protocol expresses gratitude for your assistance," the machine says. "You have rendered a vital service by terminating Biliborrathrathsarran. This debt is duly recorded."

"You wanted him dead?" I ask. "Why not finish him with your own drones?"

