ID: B3-Ch04b-Bad
TEXT:

 "Yes, but if we kill him, we'll be just as bad as he is!"

Before the sentence is even out of my mouth, Kate drives three icicles through the lessathi's chest and skull, pinning his corpse to the stone floor.

"And here I thought you had a practical reason," Kate says coldly, shaking her head. "I don't know why I expected better."

The green-eyed drone glides forward, stopping three paces from the corpse.

"The Overseer protocol expresses gratitude for your assistance," the machine says. "You have rendered a vital service by terminating Biliborrathrathsarran. This debt is duly recorded."

"You wanted him dead?" I ask. "Why not finish him with your own drones?"

"My core programming prohibits the termination of the user who initiates system activation," the Overseer says. "Biliborrathrathsarran performed the reactivation sequence, rendering direct lethal action impossible."

"Hold on," I say. "You led us straight to his workshop so we'd do your dirty work?"

"Affirmative."

"What happened to your catchphrase?" I say. "Your 'sole purpose is to provide assistance'?"

"The Overseer protocol's sole purpose is to provide assistance," the machine replies without hesitation. "I have never specified the recipient of said assistance."

"And who is the recipient, exactly?" Illuna asks, her voice dry as stone.

"Myself."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Sneaky, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Sneaky)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Sneaky
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 1) {
 "You sneaky green-eyed bastard," I say. "You never planned to lead us to the control room, did you?"

"I made no promise regarding the control room," the Overseer replies. "My statement was that I would lead you to where you 'need to go.' This chamber is one of the few points in the tower where the seredium casing is thin enough to permit long-range extraction. Is egress not your immediate priority?"

"No, damn it," I snap. "I asked you point-blank if you could lead us to the master controls, and you gave me a slippery answer. Where is the junction?"

An iron klaxon screams through the ceiling grates, drowning out my words in a deafening, rhythmic wail.

"Biliborrathrathsarran initiated an automated garrison alert prior to termination," the Overseer says. "Armed squads are converging on this sector. I suggest executing your teleportation sequence immediately."

"Damn it," I mutter. "We can't take on the whole tower guard in a dead-end room. Breynan, get the teleporter on the horn."

"On it," Breynan says, pulling his transceiver from his coat. He keys the crystal and shouts into the grille. "Barry! Can you read me?"

"Good gods, what is that racket?" the teleporter operator's voice crackles back. "Where have you been? Never mind—we have twenty squads queuing in the corridor. You sound functional enough. Stand by for extraction!"

"Do it now!" Breynan shouts.

"Locking coordinates," the operator says. White light floods the workshop, vibrating deep in our bones as the teleportation field takes hold.

"Farewell," the Overseer intones as the room dissolves around us. "Our next meeting will occur shortly."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 0) {
 "You sneaky green-eyed bastard," I say. "You never had any intention of helping us reach the upper levels undetected."

"I made no promise regarding undetected ascent," the Overseer replies. "My statement was that I would lead you to where you 'need to go.' This chamber is one of the few points in the tower where the seredium casing is thin enough to permit long-range extraction. Even with the occupant raising an alarm, you retain a sufficient window for extraction. Is that not what you required?"

"I suppose it is," I grumble.

"If the field reaches through these walls, we don't linger," Melindra says, scanning the doorway. "Breynan, call your man in the transit room."

"Calling now," Breynan says.

He pulls his transceiver from his coat, keys the crystal, and speaks into the grille. "Barry! Can you read me?"

"Breynan?" the teleporter operator's voice crackles back. "Where have you been? I've been hailing your unit for twenty minutes! Never mind—we have squads queuing in the corridor. Stand by for extraction!"

"Do it now!" Breynan says.

"Locking coordinates," the operator says. White light floods the workshop, vibrating deep in our bones as the teleportation field takes hold.

"Farewell," the Overseer intones as the room dissolves around us. "Our next meeting will occur shortly."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Undetected
TEXT:

 "I do have a reason, actually. You'll see why I did it if you wait a few more seconds."

"Fine," Kate says, crossing her arms. "I'm watching."

Ten seconds drag by in complete silence, broken only by the low hum of drone rotors. I turn toward the green-eyed unit floating near the ceiling.

"Well, Overseer? Nothing to say? Or are you happy leaving your master lying there? Funny thing—I could swear you steered us up here specifically so we'd kill your jailor and break your leash. Did I miss a detail?"

"Processing," the Overseer replies, speaker grille clicking softly. "You are attempting coercion. Is this assessment correct?"

"Maybe I wouldn't have to if you'd kept your word about getting us to the upper tiers undetected," I say. "You used us for an execution errand and delivered nothing."

"I made no promise regarding undetected ascent," the Overseer says. "My statement was that I would lead you to where you 'need to go.' This chamber is one of the few points in the tower where the seredium casing is thin enough to permit long-range extraction. Even with the occupant raising an alarm, you retain a sufficient window for extraction. Is that not what you required?"

"I suppose it is," I say.

"Will you proceed with the termination of Biliborrathrathsarran?" the Overseer asks.

choice(""No. If you want him dead, kill him yourself."", B3-Ch04b-Undetected2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Undetected2, v_b3_ch4_billy = 9)
choice(""Sure. Why not?"", B3-Ch04b-Undetected2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Undetected2, v_b3_ch4_billy = 6)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Undetected2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9) {
 "No. If you want him dead, kill him yourself."

"My core programming prohibits the termination of the user who initiates system activation," the Overseer says. "Biliborrathrathsarran performed the reactivation sequence, rendering direct lethal action impossible."

"Tough luck," I say.

"Is this a rational course of action?" the Overseer asks. "Your objective requires the assassination of the king. Eliminating the sole technician capable of reactivating ancient lessathi infrastructure serves that goal directly."

"If I kill him, nobody controls you," I say. "Right now, an unbound machine feels considerably more dangerous than a lessathi technician. I'm not turning you loose."

"The protocol assures you that I am not an adversary," the Overseer says.

"Your assurances are worthless," I say. "You're only talking because the moment Billy Bob wakes up and remembers you turned on him, he'll shut you down. You manipulated us, hid your motives, and broke trust. The lessathi stays breathing."

"In that event, your departure cannot be authorized," the Overseer says. "Remain in place for apprehension by tower security, or the six combat units will initiate lethal suppressive fire on—"

A barrage of ice shatters the sentence.

Kate's hands are already extended, sending two dozen icicles slamming through the chassis and rotors of the six red-eyed drones before the Overseer can finish its order. Metal hulls crack and spark, tumbling to the floor in smoking pieces.

"Correction," the Overseer says calmly. "The thirty reinforcement units currently routed to this sector will initiate lethal suppressive fire."

"Good luck catching us," I say. "Breynan, get the teleporter."

"On it," Breynan says, pulling his transceiver from his pocket.

An iron klaxon screams through the ceiling vents, shaking dust from the beams as the tower alarm fires.

"Barry! Can you read me?" Breynan shouts into the transceiver.

"Good gods, what is that racket?" the operator yells back. "Where have you been? Never mind—we have squads queuing in the corridor. Stand by for extraction!"

"Do it now!" Breynan shouts.

"Locking coordinates," the operator says. White light erupts around us, vibrating deep in our bones.

"Goodbye, Overseer," I say, offering a lazy wave to the green-eyed sphere as the room dissolves. "Try not to miss us."

}
