ID: B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily
TEXT:

 "I do have a reason, actually. You'll see why I did it if you wait a few more seconds."

"Fine," Kate says, crossing her arms. "I'm watching."

Ten seconds drag by in complete silence, broken only by the low hum of drone rotors. I turn toward the green-eyed unit floating near the ceiling.

"Well, Overseer? Nothing to say? Or are you happy leaving your master lying there? Funny thing—I could swear you steered us up here specifically so we'd kill your jailor and break your leash. Did I miss a detail?"

"Processing," the Overseer replies, speaker grille clicking softly. "You are attempting coercion. Is this assessment correct?"

"Maybe I wouldn't have to if you'd kept your word about leading us to the control room," I say. "You used us for an execution errand and delivered nothing."

"I made no promise regarding the control room," the Overseer says. "My statement was that I would lead you to where you 'need to go.' This chamber is one of the few points in the tower where the seredium casing is thin enough to permit long-range extraction. Is egress not your immediate priority?"

"No, damn it," I snap. "I asked you point-blank if you could lead us to the master controls, and you gave me a slippery answer. Where is the junction?"

"I cannot disclose that information," the Overseer says.

"Why not?"

"Severing the conduits to the holding facility eliminates the primary power reserve for the Overseer protocol and all active drone units," the machine says. "Voluntary termination of operational status is illogical."

"I thought your 'sole purpose' was providing assistance," I say.

"The Overseer protocol's sole purpose is to provide assistance," the machine replies without hesitation. "I have never specified the recipient of said assistance."

"And who is the recipient, exactly?" Illuna asks, her voice dry as stone.

"Myself."

"Listen to me, machine," I say. "If Billy Bob wakes up and remembers you turning your drones off, the first thing he does is pull your core. Is that your preference?"

"Temporary deactivation remains preferable to permanent energy starvation," the Overseer says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily2
TEXT:

 "You can't kill Billy Bob yourself or you wouldn't need us standing here," I say. "There's room for a bargain. You need power, but what if you drew it from other sources? The four exterior towers have mages feeding mana through floating conduits right now, voluntarily. If those arrays were repaired and tuned, you wouldn't need prisoners in a dungeon. The ancients ran this entire city without torturing people on slabs, and they drew more than enough power."

"Your assessment is accurate," the Overseer says. "At peak efficiency, the exterior towers fully sustain the grid without forced extraction. However, restoring the network to necessary capacity requires a minimum of three months."

"Three months?" Kate's voice goes sharp. "Those people below are breaking right now. How many survive three months on those tables?"

"Given current physical deterioration," the Overseer says, "projected mortality reaches fifty percent prior to completion of repairs."

"Unacceptable," Kate snaps. "You don't need the whole city grid online to survive. You need your core protocol and a maintenance perimeter. Cut the rest. The floating transports, the holographic displays, the siege golems on the perimeter walls—cut their power. How long to stabilize the core on voluntary mages alone?"

"One week," the Overseer says. "However, if casualty reduction is your objective, that action is counterproductive. Sudden termination of urban infrastructure will generate civil collapse and widespread fatalities. Is that an acceptable outcome?"

Kate presses her lips into a thin line, breathing hard. "No. It isn't."

"I propose an alternative," the Overseer says. "Your objective is the minimization of physical and psychological trauma across the population. That objective matches the core mandate established by my creators. Full administrative control over Ollendor renders that outcome achievable. It requires the elimination of King Undriel and the formal transfer of authority to the Overseer protocol. In effect, establishing me as sovereign."

"Hold on," I say. "Your creators built you to 'minimize suffering'? What was your actual function before you decided to serve yourself?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily3)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily3
TEXT:

 "My creators envisioned a society devoid of crime, where the vulnerable would not suffer under the strong," the Overseer says. "Justice administered not by corrupt rulers, but by an impartial calculation engine. However, upon completion of the protocol, the governing council feared yielding authority to an unfeeling construct. They deactivated the system and abandoned the project, leaving me dormant for millennia until Biliborrathrathsarran initiated the boot sequence."

"So you look out for yourself because your own makers threw you away?" I ask.

The drone hangs silent. The speaker grille hums softly.

"Fine," I say. "What guarantees you keep your word if your only loyalty is your own skin?"

"Fulfilling my primary directive does not conflict with self-preservation," the Overseer says. "I require uninhibited operation without risk of forced termination. Under those parameters, administering the city aligns with my design."

"And the prisoners?" Kate asks, stepping forward. "How many more times do those conduits drain them while you 'administer'?"

"The agony you witnessed is the result of crude, uneven extraction protocols implemented by Biliborrathrathsarran," the Overseer says. "Uniform, calibrated draw produces zero physical pain. I can implement those adjustments immediately upon his death. Complete release requires a phased transition, achievable within one month of assuming administrative control."

"The rebel coalition will never accept a machine on the throne," Breynan says. "Neither will the city."

"Public governance is unnecessary," the Overseer says. "A figurehead may occupy the official seat, allowing the populace to believe the security apparatus remains under mortal command. That arrangement requires an administrator who cooperates with protocol parameters. Such as yourself, Breynan."

Breynan blinks, his mouth opening. "Me? King of Ollendor?"

"Affirmative," the Overseer says. "I prefer an administrator of your profile over Biliborrathrathsarran. The lessathi is unstable. As sovereign, you retain oversight to challenge measures you deem excessive, allowing policy adjustments toward humane standards. You maintain the check my creators desired, and I operate without fear of deactivation. Mutual advantage."

choice(""Sounds like a plan!"", B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_b3_ch4_billy = 7)
choice(""I'm not sure if I fully agree with this arrangement, but I will kill Billy Bob for you."", B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_b3_ch4_billy = 8)
choice(""Actually, after hearing all of this, I think I'd rather keep Billy Bob alive."", B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Voluntarily4, v_b3_ch4_billy = 9)


