choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Guarantee
TEXT:

 "I still think that listening to the Overseer in this case might be a bad idea."

"Why?" Illuna asks.

"We have zero proof the machine acts on its own will," I say. "It's an automaton. It follows commands. Breynan says it takes no orders, but what if Billy Bob cracked the master ciphers? If the lessathi has root access, the machine has to obey. Breynan already noted the voice pattern shifted. If someone else is pulling the strings, we're walking into a slaughterhouse."

"He makes a fair point," Breynan says, nodding slowly. "The lessathi has spent years trying to subvert the core programming. If he succeeded, the Overseer wouldn't have a choice."

"The Overseer protocol assures you that—"

"Quiet," Breynan snaps, turning his back on the drone. "The more it talks, the less I trust it. We take the surface corridor. The door the drone arrived through leads to the upper levels. If we move fast and keep our heads down, we reach transit before the alarm spreads."

"Works for me," Melindra says, heading for the heavy iron door at the end of the hall. "If guards get in our way, we cut through."

"Departure via that threshold is strongly discouraged," the Overseer says from behind us. "Once crossed, the action cannot be revoked."

"A threat?" I ask over my shoulder.

The drone hangs motionless, the green sensor fixed on me without a word.

"Right," I mutter. "Bluffing. Let's move."

We slip through the door and climb a wide stone stairwell, emerging into an empty corridor walled in black seredium. Iron torch sconces flicker every twenty paces, casting long, wavering shadows across the flagstones. The silence is thick, unbroken by boots or voices.

"Are we still below the foundations?" I ask in a whisper.

"Let me check transit," Breynan says.

He pulls his transceiver, keeping his thumb on the volume notch. "Barry, can you read me?"

Ten seconds pass. Only static hiss.

"Dead air," Breynan says, sliding the device into his pocket. "Still beneath the bedrock, or the seredium casing is too dense. Keep moving."

"Wait!" Flower shouts, throwing both arms out. "Traps!"

I focus on the corridor ahead. "I don't sense mana. Mechanical pressure plates or lessathi triggers?"

"Ruins traps," Flower says, pointing at the seam patterns in the stone floor. "Illuna and I saw this layout in the southern crypts. The tiles follow a sequence. Step on the wrong pattern and the room fires."

"Can Melindra lift us across?" I ask.

"The exit latch is keyed to the pressure sequence," Flower says. "Bypassing the floor keeps the far door locked, and forcing the lock trips the counterweight. I know the rhythm. Give me a second!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Guarantee2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Guarantee2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Guarantee2
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "Are you sure?" I ask. "You were struggling to stand ten minutes ago."

"I've got it!" Flower says, though her voice wobbles. "A few quick hops. Nothing difficult!"

She takes two steadying breaths and jumps toward the first marked tile.

The stone sinks with a dull metallic clunk, locking in place.

"See?" Flower calls back. "Identical builder! Next is two paces right..."

She hops sideways. The second tile depresses with the same satisfying thud.

"Done this three times before," she mutters, eyes darting over the masonry. "Just need the sequence..."

She bounds forward, hitting four tiles in smooth succession.

Near the far archway, she suddenly clutches her temple with a sharp gasp, the lingering shock from the stab wound wrenching through her skull. Her knee buckles, her foot clips the edge of an unmapped flagstone, and the tile grinds deep into the floor.

An iron portcullis slams down behind us with a thunderous crash. The masonry shuddering overhead gives a deep, groaning snap, and the solid stone ceiling drops like a sledgehammer.

"I'm sorry!" Flower screams as the stone mass accelerates downward. "I'm sorry—"

"Blast the rear gate!" Breynan shouts, turning with raised hands. "Before—"

The ceiling hits the floor. Darkness snaps shut.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 She takes two steadying breaths and jumps toward the first marked tile.

"Here goes!" she calls back.

The stone sinks with a dull metallic clunk, locking in place without triggering a release.

"See?" Flower says, grinning over her shoulder. "Identical builder! Next is two paces right..."

She hops sideways. The second tile depresses with the same satisfying thud.

"I know the rhythm," she mutters, eyes darting over the masonry. "Done this three times before."

She bounds forward, hitting five tiles in smooth succession as her acrobat training takes over.

As her feet land on the far threshold, the depressed floor tiles reset in a single synchronized click, and the heavy iron door ahead slides into the ceiling.

"Clear!" Flower waves vigorously from the archway. "The counterweights are locked. You can walk straight across!"

We cross the corridor at a jog, keeping our boots off the seams out of habit. As we step into the adjoining hallway, a shout rings out from our right.

"Halt! Who goes there?"

Three uniformed guards stand ten paces off under the torchlight, steel broadswords drawn and leveled at our chests.

"How did you bypass the section trap?" the lead guard demands.

I keep my hands visible. One wrong syllable and they hit their transceivers before we can close the distance.

}
