ID: B3-Ch04b-Tasked
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch4_deception,1) if (v_bluff >= 4)
set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,5) if (v_bluff < 4)
set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,1) if (v_bluff >= 4)
achievement("A master of deception",v_ac_b3_ch4_deception)
#if(v_bluff < 4) {
 

"We are the new guards," I say. "We came here to relieve you of your duties."

The lead guard scoffs, eyeing my trail clothes, Flower's acrobatic tunic, and Melindra's silk outfit. "New guards? You take us for blind fools."

He taps the brass transceiver pinned to his collar and shouts:

"Intruder alert! Sound the alarm right now!"

The sector klaxon howls to life, red warning lamps pulsing overhead. The three guards advance in a fighting wedge, broadswords raised.

"You're all coming with us," one shouts. "Drop your weapons!"

Before their boots cover two paces, six tendrils of water whip up from the damp stone, coiling tight around their ankles and yanking backward with brutal force. Their feet fly up; the backs of their skulls crack hard against the flagstones, knocking all three out cold.

"I should have done this the moment we opened that door..." Illuna mutters.

"No time to second-guess," Breynan says. "The alarm is running. We move before the sector seals."

"Garrison response is converging from the east wing," Melindra says, eyes unfocused as she tracks auras through the masonry. "Take the west corridor."

"West it is," Breynan says.

We turn left, sprinting for the far archway. Melindra points us toward an ascending spiral stairwell deeper in the sector. We take two sharp rights, hit the stairs at a dead run, and take the risers three at a time as the distant thunder of armored boots echoes up the shaft behind us.

}
#if(v_bluff >= 4) {
 

"We are the new guards," I say. "We came here to relieve you of your duties."

The lead guard blinks, his gaze drifting from my leather vest to Flower's trapeze tunic, then across Melindra's silk pajamas. "New guards? Where are your uniforms?"

"Uh... we forgot them at... home?..." I say.

"Sounds plausible," the guard says, nodding with total sincerity. "How did you get past the checkpoint without logging on the roster?"

"It's because umm... you see, the thing is that we actually fell asleep in the prisoners' room the other day and we just woke up a few minutes ago," I say. "You probably weren't here when we came in the first time."

The three guards exchange glances, completely unbothered.

"Their story checks out," the leader says, sheathing his sword. "Come on, let's go grab a beer before we head home. Maybe with a little luck we'll still find some tavern open at this hour."

The three men turn around and stroll down the eastern hallway at a leisurely pace, chatting about ale.

"That was incredible..." Kate says, as soon as the three guards are out of earshot. "Did you see how quickly they believed that obvious lie? It's like those men completely lost their common sense the minute they heard Barry speak!"

"I don't understand what happened," Leila writes in floating blue script. "Barry's aura was calm, but anyone could have seen that he wasn't telling the truth just from the way he talked. If you add the fact that the reasons he gave were completely ridiculous, I'm starting to think that calming the aura is not the only thing that this 'Bluff' stat does."

"Did neither of you notice how Barry's aura enveloped the three guards while he was talking?" Melindra says. "I'm no expert, but I'm guessing that must have had something to do with their temporary lapse in judgment. Still, I really doubt that this technique could work on mages, or on anyone reasonably competent in controlling their auras, so Barry, maybe next time you might want to consider telling a lie that is at least somewhat believable. Wouldn't you agree?"

"Right, right," I say. "I got a little carried away, there. My bad."

"So, which way should we go?" Flower asks.

"Well, the guards went to the right, so that's likely the way that leads to the exit," Breynan says. "We want to avoid going there unless we're prepared to fight every single guard in this place, so we should probably go to the left."

"Fair enough," Melindra says. "To the left it is."

We all turn left and we keep walking until we reach a door at the end of the corridor. Once we open the door and step into the next hallway, Melindra tells us that she has detected a staircase leading upwards somewhere further down the hall. We take a right, then another right, and when we reach the stairs we begin to climb them slowly, while we rely on Melindra to tell us if there are any guards following us or not.

We manage to get up the stairs without any problems, and then we traverse another long corridor before we begin heading for the next staircase that leads to one of the upper levels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Hearing) if (v_bluff < 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Blades, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Blades) if (v_bluff >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Cleaners
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,5) if (v_bluff < 2)
set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,2) if (v_bluff >= 2)
#if(v_bluff < 2) {
 

"We're the new cleaners!" I say. "We were sent here to wash the prisoners downstairs."

The lead guard narrows his eyes, surveying our blades and travel gear. "The new cleaners, huh?... Please excuse me while I communicate this information to my colleagues."

He taps the brass transceiver pinned to his collar and shouts:

"Intruder alert! Sound the alarm right now!"

The hallway alarm howls to life, red warning lamps flaring in the ceiling grates. The three guards advance in a fighting wedge, blades raised.

"You're all coming with us," one of the guards says. "You'd better not be trying to resist."

Before any of them have the time to react, six water whips appear in mid-air in front of the three men, quickly coiling themselves around each of their ankles and pulling their legs from under them, causing them to hit the backs of their heads against the ground and to lose their consciousness.

"I should have done this the moment we opened that door..." Illuna says.

"Don't worry about it," Breynan says. "I'm sure we still have time to find our way out of here until the rest of the guards organize themselves to come greet us."

"The majority of the guards seem to be coming from the right side," Melindra says. "I suggest that we head to the left."

"Good thinking," Breynan says. "Let's move."

We all turn left and we make a run for the door at the end of the corridor. Once we open the door and step into the next hallway, Melindra tells us that she has detected a staircase leading upwards somewhere further down the hall. We take a right, then another right, and when we reach the stairs we begin to climb them as fast as we can, as we can hear the faint hurried steps of several guards from behind, who are slowly closing in on us.

}
#if(v_bluff >= 2) {
 

"We're the new cleaners!" I say. "We were sent here to wash the prisoners downstairs."

The guard looks us over, frowning. "The new cleaners, huh?... Would you mind telling us then how exactly you managed to get inside there, given that we've been here all day and we never saw you walk in?"

"Uh... I mean... have you even seen how many people there are down there?" I say. "We've been busy washing them since yesterday!"

"I suppose you do have a point," the guard says. "Very well. In that case, we'll let you go home. I'm sure you must be tired."

"Yes, yes," I say. "Very tired. Come on, everyone, let's go!"

"Where do you think you're going?" the guard says, when he sees us heading to the left.

"To the... exit?" I say.

"The exit's that way," the guard says, as he points towards the right side of the corridor. "Don't get lost now."

"Oh right, it was that way!" I say. "Sorry, it's our first time here, so we're getting a little confused with all these identical-looking corridors. Alright everyone, let's go to the right!"

"That was not a bad bluff, Barry," Leila writes with a smile, while we're moving away from the guards. "The points you spent in that stat were not wasted."

As we keep advancing through the corridor, we eventually reach another door. Before we open it, Melindra stops us to give us a few pointers.

"There are some guards out there, but they're pretty far away from the door," she says. "Kate and Flower, I see that you're both suppressing the magic from your auras, but that's not enough if you want to avoid being detected by someone with keen magical sense. You need to suppress your auras completely, like Illuna. Can you do that?"

"I think so..." Kate says.

"We'll try!" Flower says.

"And the same goes for everyone else too," Melindra says. "Try to keep your auras as suppressed as possible, and with a little luck, we might be able to walk past those guards unnoticed."

"Suppress my aura," I say. "Right."

I try to conceal my aura as well as I can, using the breathing exercises that I learned from Illuna two weeks ago, but I only manage to partially hide it.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Hearing) if (v_bluff < 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Cleaners2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Cleaners2) if (v_bluff >= 2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Business
TEXT:

 "It's none of your business," I say.

"None of my business, huh?" the man who addressed us earlier says. "Why don't you please excuse me for a moment while I have a little chat with one of my colleagues?"

He taps the brass transceiver pinned to his collar and shouts:

"Intruder alert! Sound the alarm right now!"

The hallway alarm howls to life, red warning lamps flaring in the ceiling grates. The three guards advance in a fighting wedge, blades raised.

"You're all coming with us," one of the guards says. "You'd better not be trying to resist."

Before any of them have the time to react, six water whips appear in mid-air in front of the three men, quickly coiling themselves around each of their ankles and pulling their legs from under them, causing them to hit the backs of their heads against the ground and to lose their consciousness.

"I should have done this the moment we opened that door..." Illuna says.

"Don't worry about it," Breynan says. "I'm sure we still have time to find our way out of here until the rest of the guards organize themselves to come greet us."

"The majority of the guards seem to be coming from the right side," Melindra says. "I suggest that we head to the left."

"Good thinking," Breynan says. "Let's move."

We all turn left and we make a run for the door at the end of the corridor. Once we open the door and step into the next hallway, Melindra tells us that she has detected a staircase leading upwards somewhere further down the hall. We take a right, then another right, and when we reach the stairs we begin to climb them as fast as we can, as we can hear the faint hurried steps of several guards from behind, who are slowly closing in on us.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Hearing)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Cleaners2
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,0) if (v_bluff < 3)
set(v_b3_ch4_tasked,4) if (v_bluff >= 3)
#if(v_bluff < 3) {
 

No matter how hard I try, I just can't seem to be able to fully conceal it.

"Hmm..." Melindra says. "Most of you are doing alright, but I'm not really sure about Barry. I can't say that his aura is all that hidden right now. Oh well... I guess we'll just have to hope that the guards from the next corridor aren't all that good at detecting auras. Let's give it a shot."

We open the door and we begin heading to the left, in the opposite direction from where the guards are stationed. Just as we take our first steps, we hear a man's shout coming from very far behind us.

"I'm not sure who exactly you're supposed to be, but you have three seconds to identify yourself before I sound the alarm," we hear the faint sound of the man's shout. "I can sense your aura from all the way over here, and it doesn't feel like the aura of anyone I know."

"Crap," I say in a low voice. "What should I do? Should I tell him that I'm one of the cleaners?"

"Too late," we hear the man shouting, and soon afterwards a very loud noise echoes through the hall, as we are sensing more than a few guards moving in our direction.

"So much for being stealthy," Melindra says. "I guess we'll have to make a run for it. There are some stairs further down this corridor. They should help us get to one of the upper levels. Let's hurry."

We quicken our pace and we follow Melindra until we reach the end of the corridor. We then take a left and we soon get to the staircase that she mentioned earlier. As we are climbing the stairs, we can hear the faint hurried steps of some of the guards from behind, who are slowly closing in on us.

}
#if(v_bluff >= 3) {
 

When I try again, I realize that I'm suddenly getting the same feeling that I got when I was lying to those guards earlier. Is this also the Bluff stat's doing?

"Hey, that's not half-bad, Barry," Melindra says. "I didn't know you could conceal your aura so well. Alright, now that everyone is ready, let's move on."

We open the door and we begin heading to the left, in the opposite direction from where the guards are stationed. The sentries pay us no mind, our concealed auras slipping past their senses. Melindra tells us that she has detected a staircase leading upwards somewhere further down the hall, so we keep moving forward and then we take another left, continuing on our way until we finally reach the stairs that she was talking about. We then begin climbing them slowly, while we rely on Melindra to tell us if there are any guards following us or not.

We manage to get up the stairs without any problems, and then we traverse another long corridor before we begin heading for the next staircase that leads to one of the upper levels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Hearing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Hearing) if (v_bluff < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Blades, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Blades) if (v_bluff >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Hearing
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_hearing < 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_hearing >= 3)
#if(v_hearing < 3) {
 

When we reach the top of the stairs, we enter a new hallway and we begin to advance through it at a rapid pace. Crackling blue electrical arcs detonate from the walls, filling the entire hallway with a blinding grid of lightning. The electric shock surging through my body is so powerful that I barely get another second of consciousness before everything completely fades to black.

}
#if(v_hearing >= 3) {
 

Just when we're about to reach the end of the stairs, I hear the voice of a guard from very far behind us, who is talking to one of his colleagues.

"They're at the top of the stairs!" the man says. "Turn on the trap! Now!"

"Stop!" I shout at my friends who are running besides me. "Don't go any further!"

We all stop right before the end of the staircase, where we are getting a perfect view of the next corridor through an archway made of stones. Just a few seconds later, the whole hallway in front of us gets filled with magical electrical currents that can be seen with the naked eye. Judging by the way they look, I'm guessing that we would have died instantly if we hadn't stopped running when we did. After waiting for about ten more seconds, the currents finally die down, and the corridor in front of us becomes safe to cross once again.

"Are they dead?" I hear the voice of a man very far behind us.

"No, they didn't enter the corridor for some reason," says the man who ordered the activation of the trap initially. "Try turning it on again!"

"I can't turn it on right away," the other man says. "It needs to charge up a little first!"

"I just heard the guards talking about how the trap will need to charge up before they can use it again," I tell my friends. "We can go now. It's safe."

"Let's hurry, then," Kate says.

"You can hear them from that far away?..." Breynan asks me, as we're all quickly traversing the corridor with the deactivated electric trap.

"Yes, it's because of my stat device," I say. "But if we run into another one of these undetectable lessathi traps later on, we might not be so lucky next time. Melindra, isn't there a way in which you could detect these with your magical sense?"

"These are lessathi devices that are made specifically for the purpose of not being detected," Melindra says. "Even Arraka would have trouble finding these. All we can do is be really careful and hope for the best."

"I was afraid that you might say something like that," I say.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_hearing < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_hearing < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_hearing < 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Blades, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Blades) if (v_hearing >= 3)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Blades
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_tasked != 2 && v_b3_ch4_tasked != 3 && v_b3_ch4_tasked != 5) {
 "Breynan, try transit again," Melindra says as we hurry down the corridor. "The seredium might be thinner on this tier."

"Still nothing," Breynan says, thumbing the transceiver. "Static on all channels. Maybe if we reach the upper—"

A brass speaker horn bolted to the masonry squawks to life with metallic distortion.

"Attention, tower security," Billy Bob's nasal voice blares through the grille. "Sensors show unauthorized personnel ascending toward the upper levels. Sector B98-6C perimeter defenses are active. Converge on the landing and eliminate them. Do not allow them to slip the perimeter!"

A piercing klaxon shrieks through the corridor. Twenty paces ahead, the masonry groans. Heavy iron plates retract in the floor, walls, and ceiling, unleashing a blinding forest of whirring steel saw blades that grind together in overlapping arcs. The entire hallway leading to the staircase becomes an impenetrable storm of spinning metal.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_tasked == 2 || v_b3_ch4_tasked == 3 || v_b3_ch4_tasked == 5) {
 "Breynan, try transit again," Melindra says as we hurry down the corridor. "The seredium might be thinner on this tier."

"Still nothing," Breynan says, thumbing the transceiver. "Static on all channels. Maybe if we reach the upper—"

A brass speaker horn bolted to the masonry squawks to life with metallic distortion.

"Attention, tower security," Billy Bob's nasal voice blares through the grille. "Sensors confirm the intruders are ascending toward the upper levels. Sector B98-6C perimeter defenses are active. Converge on the landing and eliminate them. Do not allow them to slip the perimeter!"

Twenty paces ahead, the masonry groans. Heavy iron plates retract in the floor, walls, and ceiling, unleashing a blinding forest of whirring steel saw blades that grind together in overlapping arcs. The entire hallway leading to the staircase becomes an impenetrable storm of spinning metal.

}
 "How do we clear that?" Breynan asks, staring at the whirling teeth.

"Can Flower incinerate the bearings?" I ask.

"Those blades are tempered elven alloy and heavily warded," Illuna says. "Raw fire will only melt our own boots."

"I could freeze the drive axles," Kate says, examining the gears. "The ice won't hold under that torque, but it might buy five seconds to slip through."

"What about Rose?" Flower asks, perking up. "She could freeze the whole world while we run past!"

"Last time we spoke, Rose said she had barely any control over her time weaving," I say. "We can't bet our lives on an unstable spell."

"She had a clear premonition when we were attacked below," Illuna counters. "Her grasp may be advancing faster than she admits. We are high enough in the tower that seredium interference won't block the transceiver to Thilias. Reaching out is worth the attempt."

choice("Try to contact Rose.", B3-Ch04b-Contact, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Contact)
choice("Suggest that it would be better if Kate froze the spinning blades instead.", B3-Ch04b-Froze, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Froze)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Froze
TEXT:

 "Rose's power is too volatile," I say. "If her concentration breaks for one second while we're in the teeth, we're minced meat. We use Kate's ice. At least we control the timer."

"If Kate is confident, I will not dispute it," Illuna says. "I merely pointed out the alternative."

"I'll coat only the gears and blades," Kate says, stepping forward. "Keep the floor dry so nobody slips."

She thrusts her palms forward. A roaring blast of subzero frost surges down the corridor, encasing the spinning steel discs in dense, crystalline sheets of rime. The screech of grinding metal chokes into a sudden, rigid halt.

"Move!" Melindra shouts. "Go!"

We dive into the narrow gap between the frozen blades, ducking and contorting through the razor-edged maze. Halfway across, a sickening crack echoes through the frost.

The ice shatters under the drive gears' torque.

Before anyone can take another breath, hundreds of steel teeth spin back to life at full speed, tearing through flesh and bone in a blind storm of red.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Contact
TEXT:

 "You're right," I say. "We try Rose. Kate, throw up an ice barrier behind us in case the patrol catches up."

"On it," Kate says, already conjuring a thick wall of blue-white frost across the rear passage.

I pull my transceiver, dial Rose's frequency, and tap the activating crystal.

"Rose! Can you read me?"

"Barry!" Rose's voice comes through, bright and clear. "I can hear you! Did you make it out of the lower tunnels?"

"Mostly," I say. "We're pinned down in the tower and we need your help."

"My help?"

"I'll make it quick," I say. "Have you managed to trigger the time freeze on your own since Thilias?"

"I... a few times today," Rose says, hesitation in her tone. "Only for brief moments, and the control is terrible. Every time I try to hold it, my head spins and I nearly collapse. I don't know if—"

"What's your longest hold?"

"I'm not sure... maybe thirty seconds?"

"That's enough to clear the blades," I tell the group. Then into the transceiver: "Rose, we have a corridor blocked by spinning steel traps and a guard company breathing down our necks. Your weave stops the entire world, but the transit device in this city locks onto transceiver frequencies to target spells. Focus on the resonance between our transceivers. Use the link to anchor us outside your freeze while the rest of the world stops. Can you hold the world still and let us move?"

"I can try, but Barry... if my grip slips while you're inside—"

"We'll be across in fifteen seconds," I say. "Just give us the window. Ready?"

"I think so," Rose breathes. "Give me a moment..."

Ten agonizing seconds tick by. The transceiver hiss fills with heavy, ragged breathing.

"Rose? Everything alright?"

"I'm fine," Rose gasps, her voice strained and faint. "I triggered it twice, but the anchor slipped. You were frozen both times. One more try..."

"Wait, if it's tearing you up—"

"Barry! Can you hear me?" Rose's voice suddenly shouts through the crystal.

"I hear you!"

"Time is stopped! Run! Go now—I can't hold it long!"

I glance down the corridor.

The screaming blades have frozen dead in mid-rotation. The torch flames along the walls stand motionless as sculpted glass.

"Move!" I shout, slapping Breynan's arm. "Go, go, go!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Contact2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Contact2)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Contact2
TEXT:

 We sprint through the maze of halted steel, weaving past razor-sharp edges and frozen teeth with inches to spare. My shoulder grazes a motionless blade as we tumble onto the far stone landing at the foot of the upper stairs.

I bring the transceiver to my mouth. "Rose! We're across! Drop the weave!"

A dull thump rattles through the receiver, followed instantly by the deafening shriek of saw blades resuming full speed behind us.

"Rose? Rose, answer me!"

"I'm... here," Rose whispers, her voice barely audible over the static. "Just... fainted for a moment. My head is spinning... I'll rest on the floor a bit. I'm glad you made it across."

"I'm sorry," I say softly. "I pushed you too hard. Rest now. We'll call when we reach the base."

I close the channel and nod to the others. We take the ascending stairs at a rapid pace, emerging into a vaulted gallery with towering stone arches.

Breynan pulls his transceiver again. "Transit should punch through here. If Barry answers, we pull immediate extraction. Stand ready."

#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 1) {
 "We're not leaving until we locate the master controls," Kate insists.

"The control room plan died the second the alarm went off," Breynan says bluntly. "The entire tower garrison is mobilizing. We extract now or we die in a corridor."

He keys the crystal. "Barry! Can you read me?"

"Good gods, what is that racket?" the operator's voice crackles over the background klaxons. "Where have you been? Never mind—I'm locking—wait, the signal is fluctuating! What's happening on your end? Barry? Breynan?!"

"We're here!" Breynan shouts. "Lock on! Barry!"

The voice dissolves into static hiss.

Four combat drones with glowing orange optical sensors glide down from the vaulted ceiling, boxing us into a four-point perimeter. A green-eyed unit drifts down behind them, hovering just outside the cordon.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_control == 0) {
 He keys the crystal. "Barry! Can you read me?"

"Good gods, what is that racket?" the operator's voice crackles over the background klaxons. "Where have you been? Never mind—I'm locking—wait, the signal is fluctuating! What's happening on your end? Barry? Breynan?!"

"We're here!" Breynan shouts. "Lock on! Barry!"

The voice dissolves into static hiss.

Four combat drones with glowing orange optical sensors glide down from the vaulted ceiling, boxing us into a four-point perimeter. A green-eyed unit drifts down behind them, hovering just outside the cordon.

}
 "I warned you that the threshold could not be revoked," the Overseer's voice resonates from the green-eyed drone.

"What did you do?" I demand. "Are those orange units jamming the transit signal?"

"Farewell," the Overseer says, banking smoothly away toward the upper reaches.

The four orange-eyed drones flash in unison. A shimmering, semi-transparent cage of hard mana snaps into existence around our perimeter, sealing us inside a five-pace cube.

Kate, Flower, and Illuna unleash an immediate volley—ice spears, fireballs, and water whips slam into the drones at point-blank range, only to shatter against dense spherical energy shields that flare brilliant blue and absorb the impacts without a scratch.

The air inside the cage thickens instantly. Pressure mounts against my eardrums like diving into deep water, the density of ambient mana spiking so hard that breathing turns into a chore. They're pooling power for a massive synchronized cast.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Contact3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Contact3)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Contact3
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_magical_sense < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_magical_sense >= 4)
#if(v_magical_sense < 4) {
 

Kate, Flower, and Illuna hammer the shields with every spell in their repertoire, but the drones float unmoved, their orange optics glowing brighter as they channel energy into a central nexus. Melindra snaps her arms out, wrapping each of us in layered air domes to cushion the imminent blast.

The drones release the spell.

No shield could hold against that kind of synchronized devastation. A combined cascade of superheated plasma, freezing beams, and pressurized lightning detonates inside the confined cage, tearing through barriers in an instant and reducing our bodies to cinders before the sound can even reach our ears.

}
#if(v_magical_sense >= 4) {
 

The geometric perfection of the mana weaving inside the cage strikes a chord in my memory.

Years ago in a roadside tavern, an eccentric old theorist whom everyone dismissed as mad had ranted to me about the foundational flaws in academy curricula. His core thesis was that true perfection in spellcraft is impossible—because the closer a technique approaches theoretical perfection, the more rigid and predictable its structural flow becomes, making it trivial to counter. The academies laughed him out of the room, but his diagrams stayed burned into my notes.

Watching these four machines weave their combined spell, I see exactly what he meant.

They aren't improvising. They are executing textbook formulae with absolute, unflinching precision. Even with ten different spells woven together at once, the mana channels move in flawless, predictable arcs.

My pulse pounds in my temples. My mind races through the casting patterns, breaking down each junction in the flow and the exact instant of every shift. It feels as if time has slowed to a crawl, laying bare the entire architectural blueprint of their attack.

"We can cancel their spell!" I shout over the hum of gathering energy. "Every mage in the group—listen to me! When I raise my right hand, cast the simplest, fastest spell you know. Instant release, zero complexity. Don't ask questions. Just trust me!"

They exchange a split-second glance, then give a sharp nod.

I count two beats, watching the mana node align—and throw my right hand up.

Instantly, Kate conjures a tiny ice shard, Flower snaps a spark of flame, Illuna drops a splash of water, Breynan flares a weak orb of light, and Melindra spins a miniature breeze above her palm.

I drop my hand. The spells vanish.

Five seconds later, the second harmonic cycle aligns. I raise my hand.

They cast the same minor sparks in unison.

The air remains heavy, but the drones' nexus stalls, the mana channels warping as our crude, irregular pulses interfere with their pristine resonance. With Magical Sense active, I track every ripple in the ambient auras, timing each hand signal to strike the exact structural weak points in their formulae.

On the eighth hand raise, the gathered pressure snaps with the sound of a plucked steel cable. The spell collapses entirely, dissipating harmlessly into the stone walls.

The four orange-eyed drones freeze in mid-air, their eye-lights sputtering as if stunned.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_magical_sense < 4)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_magical_sense < 4)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_magical_sense < 4)
choice("Tell Melindra to cast her air currents spell on me, so I can fly and stab the drones.", B3-Ch04b-Drones1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Drones1, v_b3_ch4_drones = 1) if (v_magical_sense >= 4)
choice("Pick up some rocks from the ground and throw them at the drones.", B3-Ch04b-Drones2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Drones2, v_b3_ch4_drones = 2) if (v_magical_sense >= 4)
choice("Try to shoot the drones with my crossbow.", B3-Ch04b-Drones3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Drones3, v_b3_ch4_drones = 3) if (v_magical_sense >= 4)
choice("Tell the others to attack the drones with everything they've got while they are stunned.", B3-Ch04b-Drones4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Drones4) if (v_magical_sense >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Drones1
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_aura_hardening < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4)
#if(v_aura_hardening < 4) {
 

"Melindra, air currents on me!" I say, drawing my dagger. "I'll take their optics while they're looping!"

"Bad idea, but you're the boss," Melindra says, flicking her wrist to wrap a column of wind around my frame.

I lift off the flagstones, angling toward the nearest drone with my dagger leveled.

Before the blade can make contact, the drone's orange optic flares brilliant white. A freezing beam engulfs my chest, locking my muscles in solid ice. Before I can fall, two stone slabs materialize on either side of my head and slam together with bone-crushing force.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening >= 4) {
 

"Melindra, air currents on me!" I say, drawing my dagger. "I'll take their optics while they're looping!"

"Bad idea, but you're the boss," Melindra says, flicking her wrist to wrap a column of wind around my frame.

I lift off the flagstones, driving forward with my dagger leveled.

The nearest drone's optic flares, blasting a freezing ray straight into my chest. My hardened aura absorbs the thermal shock, turning the lethal blast into a mere chill against my skin. Before the machine can cycle its weapons, I drive my dagger repeatedly into its glass optic, twisting the steel until the rotor shudders and the metal sphere crashes to the floor.

The barrier was tuned against ranged mana, leaving the physical chassis vulnerable.

I bank toward the second unit, feinting a high thrust. It fires a gout of flame; I veer aside on the air currents, then dive in for a lethal strike.

My blade strikes an invisible physical ward, bouncing off with a metallic clatter. Instantly, two stone slabs materialize around my temples, ready to crush my skull.

"Barry, duck!"

An icicle from Kate punches clean through the drone's optic, dispelling the stone slabs and sending the smoking chassis crashing down.

"They're alternating barriers!" I yell. "One ward for physical, one for magical—they can't hold both simultaneously!"

I drop to the floor and bring up my crossbow while Kate and Illuna ready their spells. Before the remaining two drones can recalibrate, we loose a synchronized volley—crossbow bolts, ice javelins, and water lances obliterate both units in a shower of sparks and shattered alloy.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_aura_hardening < 4)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_aura_hardening < 4)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_aura_hardening < 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Stuck, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Stuck) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Drones2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch4_thrower,1) if (v_strength >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4 || v_strength >= 3)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_aura_hardening < 4 && v_strength < 3)
achievement("Rock throwing champion",v_ac_b3_ch4_thrower)
#if(v_strength >= 3) {
 

I grab a jagged chunk of masonry from the floor and whip it at the nearest drone with everything I have.

The stone tears straight through the magical ward, smashing through the glass optic with a sickening crunch. The drone loses lift and smashes onto the flagstones.

I snatch up a second rock and hurl it at the next unit.

This time, the stone rebounds off a shimmering physical barrier with a sharp clink, dropping harmlessly to the ground.

I throw another; it bounces again.

Kate immediately fires an icicle. The projectile glides straight through the physical ward and shatters the optic, dropping the second drone.

"They're alternating barriers!" I yell. "One ward for physical, one for magical—they can't hold both simultaneously!"

I bring up my crossbow while Kate and Illuna ready their spells. Before the remaining two drones can recalibrate, we loose a synchronized volley—crossbow bolts, ice javelins, and water lances obliterate both units in a shower of sparks and shattered alloy.

}
#if(v_strength < 3) {
 

I grab a jagged chunk of masonry from the floor and throw it at the nearest drone.

The rock bounces off the outer energy barrier without leaving a scratch. I throw two more in rapid succession, but both clatter harmlessly away.

"Barry, what are you doing?" Kate asks.

"Not enough momentum from down here," I say. "Melindra, lift me up!"

"Dumb move, but your funeral," Melindra says, weaving air currents around my waist.

I float into range, clutching two stones. From twelve feet, I throw: bounced. From six feet: bounced again.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening >= 4 && v_strength < 3) {
 As I cock my arm for a third throw, the drone blasts a freezing ray into my chest. My hardened aura absorbs the thermal shock, turning the lethal beam into a mere chill.

I drop the stones and pull my dagger, driving the blade straight through the physical gap in its ward. Three violent stabs into the optic shatter the core, sending the unit tumbling down.

I bank toward the next drone, feinting a high thrust. It fires a gout of flame; I veer aside, then strike.

My blade rebounds off a physical ward. Two stone slabs materialize around my temples.

"Barry, duck!"

Kate's icicle punches clean through the optic, shattering the unit and dispelling the slabs.

"They're alternating barriers!" I yell. "One ward for physical, one for magical—they can't hold both simultaneously!"

I drop to the floor and bring up my crossbow while Kate and Illuna ready their spells. Before the remaining two drones can recalibrate, we loose a synchronized volley—crossbow bolts, ice javelins, and water lances obliterate both units in a shower of sparks and shattered alloy.

}
#if(v_aura_hardening < 4 && v_strength < 3) {
 As I cock my arm for a third throw, the drone blasts a freezing ray into my chest, locking my muscles in solid ice. Before I can fall, two stone slabs materialize around my temples and crush my skull in an instant.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Stuck, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Stuck) if (v_aura_hardening >= 4 || v_strength >= 3)
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_aura_hardening < 4 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_aura_hardening < 4 && v_strength < 3)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_aura_hardening < 4 && v_strength < 3)


ID: B3-Ch04b-Drones3
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_perception < 2)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_perception >= 2)
#if(v_perception < 2) {
 

I raise my crossbow and put a bolt into the nearest drone's optic. The iron tip strikes a shimmering barrier and bounces away, leaving the hull unmarked.

Physical attacks aren't cutting through. We have to brute-force the wards with elemental magic until the capacitors overload.

"Focus fire on their shields!" I shout. "Wear them down!"

"If you say so," Kate mutters.

Kate, Flower, Illuna, and Breynan unleash a sustained barrage of fire, ice, and light. The drones absorb the impacts without moving, but after five seconds of relentless pounding, their orange optics begin to strobe violently in high-pitched, harmonic pulses.

"Warning," all four units announce in flat synthetic unison. "Barrier integrity critical. Initiating self-destruct sequence."

"Barrier, now!" Breynan screams.

The four chassis detonate simultaneously in a concussive firestorm that obliterates the gallery and everything in it.

}
#if(v_perception >= 2) {
 

I raise my crossbow and put a bolt into the nearest drone's optic. The iron tip bounces off the barrier, but my eye catches a microscopic flicker in the orange sensor light at the instant of impact.

That didn't happen with the magical spells.

I reload and loose two bolts in rapid succession. The barrier deflects both, but the orange optic strobes visibly with each hit.

One more volley.

I cycle three fast shots into the center lens. On the third impact, the physical barrier buckles, and the iron point punches through glass and circuitry. The drone spins out and hits the stone with a heavy clatter.

I pivot to the second unit, firing three bolts into its hull.

The barrier doesn't even ripple. The orange light stays steady.

Before I can adjust, Kate fires an icicle that passes effortlessly through the physical ward, piercing the optic and dropping the machine.

"They're alternating barriers!" I yell. "One ward for physical, one for magical—they can't hold both simultaneously!"

I reload my crossbow while Kate and Illuna ready their spells. Before the remaining two drones can recalibrate, we loose a synchronized volley—crossbow bolts, ice javelins, and water lances obliterate both units in a shower of sparks and shattered alloy.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_perception < 2)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_perception < 2)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_perception < 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch04b-Stuck, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04b-Stuck) if (v_perception >= 2)


